  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Both the Wind Advisory and Flood Watch\nthat were in effect through 5 AM this morning have been allowed to\nexpire. Winds across east-central Nevada have subsided below\nadvisory levels, and the threat of flash flooding and debris flows\non burn scars due to heavy rain showers has diminished. Widespread \nshowers will continue across most of central and northeastern \nNevada through Tuesday afternoon. No updates to the forecast at \nthis time. \n\nPrevious Discussion...The upper low that absorbed the remnants\nof Hurricane Rachel and its moisture will sit atop the Great \nBasin through Tuesday, keeping showers in place and producing yet \nanother quarter to half inch of precipitation the rest of tonight\nthrough Tuesday afternoon, some of which may fall as snow as \ntemperatures drop. Most snow will be found at high elevations, \nhowever valleys in eastern Nevada could wake up to about a quarter\ninch of snow on Tuesday. Any valley snow will be short lived as \nthe low exits to the east Tuesday, allowing high temperatures to \nrise from the low 50s to the upper 60s by Thursday. Tuesday will \nalso be the final day for precipitation, with dry weather and \ncontinued warming expected through the end of the forecast.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in continuing\nshowers through Tuesday, with moderate confidence in accumulation\ntotals and snowfall potential. Moderate confidence in warming\ntemperatures and dry conditions beyond Tuesday. The only changes\nmade to the NBM output was to increase the chance of thunderstorms\nthe rest of tonight through this afternoon.",
